  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nThe synoptic pattern features a broad H5 ridge stretched across much\nof the eastern CONUS. Underneath this ridge, surface high pressure \nremains the predominant system influencing our weather pattern, \nresulting in dry weather with mostly clear skies. The anomalous \nheights of the ridge have led to seasonably warm daytime highs in the\n60s, which will continue through Sunday. However, the air mass \nremains very dry as dewpoints meander between the 20s and 30s, so we\nwill continue to observe large swings in diurnal temperatures \nthrough this weekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nThe mid level ridge and surface high pressure system get shunted \neastward as we approach the end of the weekend. A shortwave trough \nejects through the Midwest region Sunday night. While the forcing \nwill help generate some rain across the region, we won't observe a \nlarge influx of moisture ahead of this trough and associated cold \nfront. Therefore, QPF values generally remain pretty light, with most\nof the fa observing less than 0.25\" and perhaps some struggling to \neven observe measurable rainfall. \n\nMeridional flow pattern will result in the potential for more active\nweather across the Ohio Valley next week, particularly around the\nThanksgiving holiday. Models are in agreement showing a low pressure\nsystem to our south that could usher in some pcpn, perhaps even some \nsnow if thermal profiles remain cool enough to limit melting.",
